test: correct the drop-accuracy probe's end-of-list aim point

The "bottom of target" case reported a MISMATCH and the README described it
as a real limitation: drops at the bottom of a long scrolling column land a
few slots short. That diagnosis was wrong, and the probe's own comment
explained the false behaviour as a library constraint.

Measured instead of assumed. tools/dropcurve.mjs sweeps the aim point down
a target column and prints the resulting index; tools/dropgeom.mjs dumps
per-column geometry; tools/dropoffset.mjs sweeps fixed pixel offsets. The
response is monotonic and the end-of-list index is reachable, both for a
short column and for a 40-card column that genuinely scrolls (scrollHeight
3103 vs clientHeight 663). The nested-scroll-container warning is cosmetic
here, so no layout change was warranted.

The real defect was the probe's aim point. Dropping INSIDE a card means
"insert above that card", so aiming at 75% of the last card correctly
resolves to n-1. Worse, a fixed pixel offset measured from the last CARD
drifts as the list grows, because the card's height changes between runs.
Measuring from the droppable's bottom edge is stable.

droptest now passes 3/3 across repeated runs. The README limitation is
replaced with the measured result, and the three diagnostics are documented
alongside the existing scenarios.